  "name": "Qom (Toba) song and n'vike fiddle",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Music of the Guaycuruan-speaking Qom (Toba) of the Argentine Chaco and Formosa. The n'vike, a one-string fiddle with a tin or gourd resonator bowed with horsehair, carries the community's instrumental tunes, and singing ranges from older ceremonial songs to the hymns of the Qom evangelical churches that spread from the mid-twentieth century. The Coro Toba Chelaalapí of Resistencia sings in the Qom language and has released albums, and the Instituto Nacional de Musicología 'Carlos Vega' holds field recordings.",
  "recipe": "classic modern qom (toba) song and n'vike fiddle, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. group vocals. tin can ostinato nvike. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
